// Traverse the list in reverse order
traversal.traverse(nodeList.nodes, (node) -> System.out.println(node.toString()), () -> System.out.println("No Action"), true);
```

In this example, we create two lists of nodes: one forward and one backward. We then use the `NodeTraversal` class to traverse both lists and print out the node data.

**Example Use Case for NodeList**

Here's an example usage of the `NodeList` class:

```java
public static void main(String[] args) {
    NodeList nodeList = new NodeList();

    // Add some nodes
    nodeList.add(new Node("A"));
    nodeList.add(new Node("B"));
    nodeList.add(new Node("C"));

    // Traverse the list in reverse order
    System.out.println("Traversing in reverse order:");
    traversal.traverse(nodeList.nodes, (node) -> System.out.println(node.toString()), () -> System.out.println("No Action"), true);
}
```

In this example, we create a `NodeList` object and add three nodes to it. We then use the `traverse` method of the `NodeTraversal` class to traverse the list in reverse order, printing out the node data.
